WEBVTT

00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:08.590
<v Ira Glass>From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass.
<v Ira Glass>来自芝加哥WBEZ电台，这里是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。

00:00:08.590 --> 00:00:17.020
<v Ira Glass>There have been seven Peaches in the duo Peaches and Herb over the years. But it was Peaches number three-- her real name's Linda Greene-- in the biggest hit ever, singing the duet with Herb.
<v Ira Glass>多年来，“桃子与香草”二重唱组合中先后出现过七位“桃子”。但在这首史上最热门的歌曲中，与香草合唱的正是第三任“桃子”，她的真名是琳达·格林。

00:00:17.020 --> 00:00:17.170
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - PEACHES AND HERB, "REUNITED"]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - 桃子与香草，《重聚》]

00:00:17.170 --> 00:00:28.940
<v Linda Greene>(SINGING) I was a fool to ever leave your side. Me minus you is such a lonely ride.
<v Linda Greene>(演唱) 离开你身边是我太傻。没有你的我，旅途如此孤寂。

00:00:28.940 --> 00:00:49.920
<v Ira Glass>We live in a country where 3 million people have purchased copies of this song. And Peaches number three tells this story. She was on tour, and she visited this elementary school back in the day. And she has asked these little kids-- first, and second, and third graders-- what their favorite song was, and they all shouted, "Reunited." And what they said was, that song was played when my mommy and daddy got back together.
<v Ira Glass>我们生活在一个有300万人购买过这首歌的国家。第三任“桃子”讲述了这个故事。当时她正在巡演，去了一所小学。她问那些小孩子——一、二、三年级的学生——他们最喜欢的歌是什么，他们全都喊道：“《重聚》”。他们说，这首歌是在我爸爸妈妈复合时播放的。

00:00:49.920 --> 00:01:08.210
<v Ira Glass>And you know what that speaks to? There are not many songs for people who split up and get back together again. The reunited couple is a completely under-served market, right? There's "Love Is More Comfortable the Second Time Around," there's "Break Up to Make Up," and there's "Reunited." That's pretty much it. Oh, wait a second, here's the chorus.
<v Ira Glass>你知道这说明了什么吗？关于分手后又复合的人的歌曲并不多。重归于好的情侣是一个完全未被满足的市场，对吧？有《第二次爱的感觉更舒适》，有《分手为了和好》，还有《重聚》。基本上就这些了。哦，等等，副歌来了。

00:01:08.210 --> 00:01:20.480
<v Linda Greene>(SINGING) Reunited and it feels so good. Reunited 'cause we understood, there's one perfect fit--
<v Linda Greene>(演唱) 重聚了，感觉真好。重聚了，因为我们理解，我们是天生一对——

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:51.030
<v Ira Glass>Who is singing the songs for the reunited people? Who is writing their stories? Well, today on our radio show, we tried to redress this grievous, grievous oversight and tell three stories of reunions-- in each case, very unlikely reunions that were gotten to through very unusual means. Act One is a man reunited with a woman. Act Two is a man reunited with an animal. Act Three, a man reunited with a nation. Sarah Vowell tells that last story. And let's just get right to it, huh?
<v Ira Glass>谁在为重归于好的情侣唱歌？谁在书写他们的故事？好吧，今天在我们的广播节目中，我们试图弥补这一严重的疏忽，讲述三个重聚的故事——每一个都是非常不可能的重聚，通过非常不寻常的方式实现。第一幕是一位男士与一位女士重聚。第二幕是一位男士与一只动物重聚。第三幕是一位男士与一个国家重聚。莎拉·沃威尔讲述了最后一个故事。咱们直接开始吧，好吗？

00:01:51.030 --> 00:02:14.560
<v Ira Glass>Act One, "Not Your Parents' 'Parent Trap.'" This is a story of two marriages-- one unhappy, the other happy. One's an arranged marriage, the other's by their own choice. One's a big formal wedding, the other's in City Hall. One's in Iran, the other's in America. The only thing they have in common is the bride and groom, who also are the parents of Nazanin Rafsanjani, who tells our story.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，“不是你父母的‘天生一对’”。这是一个关于两段婚姻的故事——一段不幸，一段幸福。一段是包办婚姻，另一段是自由恋爱。一段是盛大的正式婚礼，另一段是在市政厅举行。一段发生在伊朗，另一段在美国。它们唯一的共同点就是新郎和新娘，也就是纳扎宁·拉夫桑贾尼的父母，她将为我们讲述这个故事。

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:31.620
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>From the day my parents met to the day they were married, it took three weeks, which wasn't so out of the ordinary in Iran in 1973. Back then, people didn't get to date. It just wasn't done. They had to leave it to their families to choose a spouse for them. They got married and hoped for the best.
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>从我父母见面到结婚，只用了三周时间，这在1973年的伊朗并不罕见。那时，人们不能自由恋爱。根本没有那种事。他们必须让家人为他们挑选配偶。他们结婚，然后祈祷一切顺利。

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:41.740
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>Now that I know how my parent's marriage began and ended, I can hear hints of how the next 30 years would go for them in the different ways they described their feelings at the very beginning. This is my mom, Mina.
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>现在我了解了父母婚姻的开始和结束，从他们描述最初感受的不同方式中，我可以察觉到未来30年他们生活走向的端倪。这是我的母亲，米娜。

00:02:41.740 --> 00:02:58.510
<v Mina Rafsanjani>I thought, he asked me, and he's serious. And I thought I'd fall in love with him. And I thought, well, I have to get married, and he was right there.
<v Mina Rafsanjani>我想，他向我求婚了，而且他是认真的。我想我会爱上他的。我心想，好吧，我必须结婚，而他正好出现在那里。

00:02:58.510 --> 00:03:06.980
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>[LAUGHS] So, wait. He was right there? Or you thought you-- or you were in love with him?
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>[笑] 等等。他正好在那儿？或者你以为你……或者你当时爱上他了？

00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:36.000
<v Mina Rafsanjani>He was right there. And I liked him a lot. And I thought then, I thought I liked him a lot. I loved him. I loved him, I guess, then. Yeah. I think. [LAUGHS] I was only 19, for god's sake. [LAUGHS]
<v Mina Rafsanjani>他就在那儿。我很喜欢他。那时我想，我真的很喜欢他。我想我当时是爱他的。我想是的。 [笑] 我才19岁，老天。 [笑]

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:37.530
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>Were you in love with Mina?
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>你爱米娜吗？

00:03:37.530 --> 00:03:38.830
<v Abbas Rafsanjani>Yeah.
<v Abbas Rafsanjani>爱。

00:03:38.830 --> 00:03:42.240
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>How did you-- tell me how you felt.
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>你当时感觉如何？告诉我。

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:55.440
<v Abbas Rafsanjani>Well, love is love. I don't know how to explain about love. When you love somebody, so you want to do everything for her.
<v Abbas Rafsanjani>爱就是爱。我不知道怎么解释爱。当你爱一个人时，你会想为她做任何事。

00:03:55.440 --> 00:04:06.480
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>This is my dad, Abbas. It was always less complicated for my dad. From the beginning, he loved my mom. And from the beginning, my mom didn't know what it meant to love him.
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>这是我的父亲，阿巴斯。对我父亲来说，事情总是没那么复杂。从一开始，他就爱着我母亲。而从一开始，我母亲就不知道爱他意味着什么。

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:28.020
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>My mom borrowed her wedding dress from a cousin. They were married in a large hall in downtown Tehran, with lots of food and dancing. It was all new and fun for my mom, until that night. There was this business of proving my mother's virginity, which meant my grandmother, my dad's mom, sat outside their room and waited for a piece of white cloth that would prove my mom was pure.
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>我母亲向表亲借了婚纱。他们在德黑兰市中心的一个大厅举行了婚礼，有大量的食物和舞蹈。这对母亲来说既新鲜又有趣，直到那天晚上。那是证明我母亲贞洁的仪式，这意味着我的祖母，也就是我父亲的母亲，坐在他们房间外，等待一块白布，以证明我母亲是纯洁的。

00:04:28.020 --> 00:04:52.640
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>My grandmother carried it downstairs and displayed it for my dad's entire family, who were there waiting. For my mom, a 19-year-old girl who had never held hands with a boy before, this was traumatic, and she didn't want to talk about it on tape. This was the beginning of her marriage and her sex life.
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>祖母把它拿下去，展示给我父亲的所有家人看，他们都在那里等着。对于一个从未和男孩牵过手的19岁女孩来说，这是一种创伤，她不想在录音中谈论这件事。这就是她婚姻和性生活的开始。

00:04:52.640 --> 00:05:11.180
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>My mom has a saying. I've heard it over and over growing up. An Iranian marriage is like going to the grocery store and buying a watermelon. You don't know what you've got, until it's too late. By the time my mom realized she wasn't happy with her watermelon, my parents were already parents.
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>我母亲有一句口头禅。我从小就听过很多遍。伊朗的婚姻就像去杂货店买西瓜。直到为时已晚，你才知道你买到了什么。当我母亲意识到她对自己的“西瓜”不满意时，我的父母已经有了孩子。

00:05:11.180 --> 00:05:22.130
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>The main issue was my father's temper. He could erupt at any time. My mother tiptoed around it. She started to shut down. She expected my dad to throw a fit about everything.
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>主要问题是我父亲的脾气。他随时可能爆发。我母亲小心翼翼地应付。她开始封闭自己。她预料到父亲会对任何事情大发雷霆。

00:05:22.130 --> 00:05:32.430
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>She has lots of stories, like the time she went to get her driver's license. In Tehran at that time, everybody failed the driver's test. So to practice, people took it several times.
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>她有很多故事，比如她去考驾照的那次。在当时的德黑兰，每个人都会挂科。所以为了练习，人们考了很多次。

00:05:32.430 --> 00:05:55.590
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>One day, my dad decided my mom should get some practice, so he drove her to Tehran's DMV and sent her inside, expecting to wait 10 minutes. But miraculously, she passed and then was shuffled into another line to take the driving test, which she also passed. In the end, what should have been a 10-minute failure ended up taking two hours. Finally, my mom emerged ecstatic with her brand new license.
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>有一天，我父亲决定让母亲练习一下，于是他开车带她去德黑兰的车管所，让她进去，以为只用等10分钟。但奇迹般地，她通过了，然后被安排到另一条队去参加路考，她也通过了。最后，本该是10分钟的挂科，结果却花了两个小时。最后，母亲拿着崭新的驾照狂喜地走了出来。

00:05:55.590 --> 00:06:19.470
<v Mina Rafsanjani>So I just walked out and I showed him my-- you know? I passed, I passed! And he started to yell at me. I told you 10 minutes! Why are you so late? You're so stupid! I had a meeting. I thought, so if you had all those things, why did you want me to go and take the test?
<v Mina Rafsanjani>我走出来向他展示我的……你知道吗？我通过了，我通过了！他开始对我大吼大叫。我告诉过你10分钟！为什么这么晚？你太笨了！我还有个会呢。我心想，既然你有那么多事，为什么还要让我去考试？

00:06:19.470 --> 00:06:27.190
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>And then after that, it's like, I always remember he would calm down, and then he would just forget that it ever happened.
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>之后，我记得他总是会平静下来，然后就假装什么都没发生过。

00:06:27.190 --> 00:06:51.500
<v Mina Rafsanjani>Yeah, he just-- nothing happened. Every time that I wanted to bring it up and to tell him, you know, you embarrassed me and you embarrassed yourself. Did you realize there was 10 people were looking at you? Your kids were looking at you? He said, you're making too big of a deal. Nothing happened. Everybody gets angry.
<v Mina Rafsanjani>是的，他就是……好像什么都没发生。每次我想提起这件事，告诉他，你知道，你让我和我自己都很尴尬。你意识到有10个人在看着你吗？你的孩子们在看着你吗？他说，你太小题大做了。什么都没发生。每个人都会生气。

00:06:51.500 --> 00:06:53.780
<v Nilofar Rafsanjani>It was kind of like living with an alcoholic.
<v Nilofar Rafsanjani>这就像和一个酒鬼住在一起。

00:06:53.780 --> 00:06:55.460
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>This is my sister, Nilofar.
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>这是我的妹妹，尼洛法。

00:06:55.460 --> 00:07:15.080
<v Nilofar Rafsanjani>Because you never knew. Like, one second we'd go out for a picnic, and it'd be sunny, and everything would be great. And literally, the camera wouldn't work. And he'd freak out, and the rest of the day would be shot to hell.
<v Nilofar Rafsanjani>因为你永远不知道。比如，前一秒我们出去野餐，阳光明媚，一切都很美好。但实际上，相机没法用。他就会抓狂，剩下的一整天都彻底毁了。

00:07:15.080 --> 00:07:33.970
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>When people asked me about my parents, I'd say, my mom doesn't love my dad, my dad loves my mom, and they're not very happy. And the only one who probably didn't see it that way was my dad. Nobody ever complained to him about his temper. And even now, he has a hard time remembering the moments that upset the rest of us.
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>当人们问起我的父母时，我会说，我妈不爱我爸，我爸爱我妈，他们过得不太开心。唯一可能不这么看的人就是我父亲。从来没有人向他抱怨过他的脾气。即使是现在，他也很难记起那些让我们其他人感到难过的时刻。

00:07:33.970 --> 00:07:55.940
<v Abbas Rafsanjani>I thought it's OK. Yeah, I thought if asking somebody your life is OK, your marriage is OK, yeah, I said, it was OK. Because as a-- I don't know. Maybe as a Iranian man, I didn't see anything wrong.
<v Abbas Rafsanjani>我觉得还可以。是的，我想如果向某人求婚你的生活还可以，婚姻还可以，是的，我说，这还可以。因为作为一个……我不知道。也许作为一个伊朗男人，我没觉得有什么不对。

00:07:55.940 --> 00:08:14.610
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>My dad wasn't just being clueless. By Iranian standards, there was nothing wrong with this marriage. Most of my mother's friends put up with a lot worse. And my mom was the first one to say that if they'd stayed in Iran, in an entire society that thought about marriage the way her Iranian friends did, my mother wouldn't have hit a breaking point.
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>我父亲并不只是迟钝。按照伊朗的标准，这段婚姻没什么不对劲。我母亲的大多数朋友忍受的情况还要糟糕得多。而我母亲是第一个说，如果他们留在伊朗，在一个看待婚姻方式与她那些伊朗朋友相同的社会里，她是不会达到崩溃点的。

00:08:14.610 --> 00:08:22.550
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>It happened when she was 40 years old. And because of complications from a medical procedure long ago in Iran, she'd had to get an emergency hysterectomy.
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>这发生在她40岁的时候。由于很久以前在伊朗做过手术的并发症，她不得不进行紧急子宫切除术。

00:08:22.550 --> 00:08:44.780
<v Mina Rafsanjani>My sex desire just gone down to the drain. And I talked to him. I said just, I really can compromise with you. You're a healthy guy. You have highly sex drive. And I'll work with it, but you need to work with me, too.
<v Mina Rafsanjani>我的性欲简直降到了谷底。我和他谈过。我说，我真的可以迁就你。你是个健康的男人。你有很强的性欲。我会配合，但你也需要配合我。

00:08:44.780 --> 00:08:50.410
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>"Work with me" meant she'd still have sex with him, but a lot less than he wanted. He said, no.
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>“配合我”意味着她仍然会和他过性生活，但次数比他想要的少得多。他说，不。

00:08:50.410 --> 00:09:32.890
<v Mina Rafsanjani>And he said, you know what? I'm still young, and this is my desire. If you wanted me not to get angry, be happy, you know, be good husband, take it or leave it. And that night, I thought, OK, I'm all yours, [LAUGHS] until my kids get out of the house. Then, I did my plan.
<v Mina Rafsanjani>他说，你知道吗？我还年轻，这是我的欲望。如果你不想让我生气，那就快乐点，你知道，做一个好妻子，要么接受，要么滚。那天晚上，我想，好吧，我属于你， [笑] 直到我的孩子们搬出去。然后，我制定了我的计划。

00:09:32.890 --> 00:09:44.720
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>When they had this conversation, I was in eighth grade. And we'd been in the US for almost nine years. I never knew this was a turning point, but my mother tells me it was the moment she started plotting her divorce.
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>当他们进行这次谈话时，我还在上八年级。我们在美国待了近九年。我从不知道这是一个转折点，但我母亲告诉我，那一刻她开始策划离婚。

00:09:44.720 --> 00:10:02.200
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>This was a huge step for her. My family has lived in Tehran since the 1880s, and in that time, no one, not one couple, has divorced. In 125 years, everyone on both sides of my parents' families has stayed married. And that's not because there were so many happy marriages.
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>这对她来说是巨大的一步。我的家族自19世纪80年代以来一直住在德黑兰，在那段时间里，没有一个人，没有一对夫妻离婚过。在125年里，我父母双方家族的每个人都维持着婚姻。这并不是因为有那么多幸福的婚姻。

00:10:02.200 --> 00:10:13.000
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>My mom plotted the divorce for six years. She waited until I left home and went to college, before she told my dad she wanted out. He was shocked. He'd had no idea this was coming.
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>我母亲策划离婚策划了六年。她等到我离家上大学后，才告诉父亲她想退出。他很震惊。他根本不知道这事要来了。

00:10:13.000 --> 00:10:29.460
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>And then, as if they wanted to make things as uncomfortable as possible, they continued to live together for six more months. My mom had no other place to go. She filed the official divorce papers without telling my father while they were living in the same house. It was a pretty grim time.
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>然后，就像他们想让事情尽可能不舒服一样，他们又在一起生活了六个月。我母亲没有别的地方可去。她在没有告诉父亲的情况下提交了正式的离婚文件，当时他们还住在同一个房子里。那是一段非常阴郁的时光。

00:10:29.460 --> 00:11:09.660
<v Abbas Rafsanjani>I couldn't sleep. I couldn't eat. And from a very small period of time, I start to drinking. And I thought, OK, this is not good. And then, I went to see the doctor, and he put myself in medication, like a Prozac or something. And that one helped me a little bit. But still, I was very, very-- I thought this, my life is done.
<v Abbas Rafsanjani>我睡不着觉。我也吃不下饭。在很短的一段时间里，我开始喝酒。我想，好吧，这不好。然后，我去看医生，他给我开了药，比如百忧解之类的。那个稍微帮了我一点。但我仍然非常非常……我想，我的人生完了。

00:11:09.660 --> 00:11:30.120
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>I was constantly worried about my father. I had no idea how he'd manage living by himself. I was still in college working as a secretary when I got a phone call one day. It was my dad sobbing. I had seen him cry once before when his father died. And that time, he cried quietly. And I could tell he didn't want me to be any part of it.
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>我一直担心我父亲。我不知道他一个人怎么生活。有一天，我还在上大学，做着秘书工作，接到了一个电话。是我父亲在抽泣。我以前只见过他哭过一次，在他父亲去世的时候。那次，他静静地哭了。我看得出来，他不想让我卷入其中。

00:11:30.120 --> 00:11:57.890
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>On the phone that day at work, I had no idea what to do. I was in a crowded office, trying to sound like I was having a casual conversation. People kept walking by, and I was trying to make my voice sound calm. Other calls kept coming in, but I couldn't put my dad on hold when he kept repeating, I can't do this, over and over again. I felt the same way talking to him-- I can't do this.
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>那天在工作中接到那个电话，我不知道该怎么办。我在一个拥挤的办公室里，努力让声音听起来像是在进行随意的交谈。人们不停地走过，我试图让我的声音听起来平静。其他电话不断打进来，但当他反复说“我做不到”时，我不能让他等待。和他谈话时，我也觉得一样——我也做不到。

00:11:57.890 --> 00:12:12.370
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>But for the first time in my life, I started spending time with him anyway, because I was scared for him. He'd come and visit me at school. I always made sure it was for breakfast. I remember thinking that nobody gets too hysterical over breakfast.
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>但这是我一生中第一次开始不管怎样都花时间陪他，因为我为他担心。他会来看我，去学校看我。我总是确保是在早餐时间。我记得我当时想，没人会在早餐时歇斯底里。

00:12:12.370 --> 00:12:16.810
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>Do you remember talking to me about Mina and the stuff during that time?
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>你还记得在那段时间和我谈论米娜和那些事情吗？

00:12:16.810 --> 00:13:02.230
<v Abbas Rafsanjani>Yeah, for the first year, I remember I talked to you about Mina. And then after one year, I tried to not talk about anything at all, because I beg her, I ask her, OK, back together. But when she said, no, I said, OK, this is never happen. It's going to be never happen. Why should I bother her? And somehow, I want to, a little bit, forget about Mina.
<v Abbas Rafsanjani>记得，第一年，我记得我向你谈论过米娜。后来一年后，我尽量不去谈任何事情，因为我恳求她，我问她，好吧，复合吧。但当她说不时，我说，好吧，这永远不会发生了。这永远不会发生了。我为什么要打扰她？不知怎的，我想，稍微忘掉米娜一点。

00:13:02.230 --> 00:13:15.970
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>While I was worried about my dad, I was excited for my mother. For 27 years, she thought my dad was the one thing holding her back. And my sister and I thought what she thought. We had this glorious, liberated woman vision for her.
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>当我在为父亲担心时，我也为母亲感到兴奋。27年来，她一直认为父亲是阻碍她前进的唯一因素。我和我妹妹也和她想的一样。我们对她有一个辉煌、解放的女性愿景。

00:13:15.970 --> 00:13:33.040
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>I pictured her coming home, relaxing with a glass of wine. I pictured her dating, throwing her head back in laughter at sophisticated jokes told by handsome men her age. I thought she'd divorce and turn into a completely different person-- a much happier person. For a very short time, I was right.
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>我想象着她回家，喝着红酒放松。我想象着她去约会，听着英俊的同龄男人讲着复杂的笑话而开怀大笑。我以为她离婚后会变成一个完全不同的人——一个更快乐的人。有很短的一段时间，我猜对了。

00:13:33.040 --> 00:13:53.440
<v Mina Rafsanjani>There was a lot of relief. Looked like somebody took a big, heavy weight off my shoulders. So I don't have to call person every two hours and tell him where am I. I don't have to cook all the time. I don't have to clean all the time. So it was me, and me, and me.
<v Mina Rafsanjani>感到如释重负。看起来有人从我肩上卸下了一个沉重的负担。所以我不需要每隔两小时就打给那个人告诉他在哪儿。我不需要一直做饭。我不需要一直打扫。所以就是我，我，还有我。

00:13:53.440 --> 00:14:16.350
<v Mina Rafsanjani>So went to, you know, coffee shop, and sit there and read the newspaper. It was such a good feeling. I never had it, because I went from my parents' house to my husband's house.
<v Mina Rafsanjani>所以我去了咖啡馆，坐在那儿读报纸。感觉真好。我以前从来没有过这种感觉，因为我从父母家搬到了丈夫家。

00:14:16.350 --> 00:14:23.760
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>She read the newspaper at a coffee shop exactly three times. She never dated-- not once. And things started getting harder.
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>她在咖啡馆读了三次报纸。她再也没有约会过——一次都没有。事情开始变得艰难起来。

00:14:23.760 --> 00:14:41.350
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>My mom called me all the time in tears. She'd talk about how much she hated my dad. And she'd tell me how lonely she was. She had all of this energy, and nobody to direct it to now that my dad was gone. My mom had always seemed so independent, but that was when she was surrounded by people who depended on her.
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>我母亲一直含着泪给我打电话。她会说她有多恨我父亲。她会告诉我她有多孤独。她有这么多精力，但现在父亲走了，没有地方发泄了。我母亲一直看起来那么独立，但那是当她被依赖她的人包围的时候。

00:14:41.350 --> 00:15:09.660
<v Mina Rafsanjani>I was so isolated. My family weren't around. My kids were so busy. And my friends were very kind, very nice, but after in two years divorce, I never, ever had experienced that they invited me with the bunch of friends. They invited me like I'm a drug dealer, and nobody have to see me. And they feed me one night, and kick me out of the door.
<v Mina Rafsanjani>我太孤立了。我的家人不在身边。我的孩子们太忙了。我的朋友们很友善，很好，但在离婚两年后，我从来没有经历过他们邀请我和一群朋友聚会。他们邀请我的时候就像我是个毒贩，没人想看到我。他们请我吃顿饭，然后把我踢出门外。

00:15:09.660 --> 00:15:24.420
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>Meanwhile, my dad was reluctantly turning into an American bachelor. He joined a gym. He had no food in his refrigerator. He was always at work at his dry cleaning business. And if he did have any free time, he spent it reading that most American of genres.
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>与此同时，我父亲正不情愿地变成一个美国单身汉。他去健身房。他的冰箱里没有食物。他一直在干洗店工作。如果他真的有空闲时间，他会花时间去读那种最典型的美国类型书籍。

00:15:24.420 --> 00:15:55.500
<v Abbas Rafsanjani>Actually, I read a lot of book about relationship between man and woman. And one is give me good information-- Men are From Mars, Women are From Venus. I want to give myself more knowledge, what I did wrong. If I want to go to have a relationship with other lady, I don't have to do something like that.
<v Abbas Rafsanjani>实际上，我读了很多关于男人和女人之间关系的书。其中一本给了我很好的信息——《男人来自火星，女人来自金星》。我想让自己获得更多知识，看看我做错了什么。如果我想和其他女士建立关系，我不能再做那种事了。

00:15:55.500 --> 00:16:23.940
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>I can't believe I'm saying this, but Men are From Mars, Women are From Venus changed my father's life. It's hard to imagine my dad sitting in his little apartment struggling with chapter titles like, "Women are Like Waves," and "Healing the Resentment Flu," but I can see why this book helped him. There's a thing in the beginning about how many marriages this book has saved and how everyone at any age is capable of change. And that's exactly what my dad needed-- someone to give him a chance.
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>我简直不敢相信我在说这话，但《男人来自火星，女人来自金星》改变了我父亲的生活。很难想象我父亲坐在他的小公寓里，费力地读着像“女人像波浪”、“治愈怨恨的流感”这样的章节标题，但我明白为什么这本书对他有帮助。书开头提到这本书挽救了多少婚姻，以及任何年龄的人都有能力改变。而这正是父亲所需要的——有人给他一个机会。

00:16:23.940 --> 00:16:54.720
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>The book offered simple advice like, as a man learns to listen and interpret a woman's feelings, communication becomes easier. If he becomes very frustrated while listening, he should not try to continue. Instead, the respectful thing to say is, I really want to hear what you are saying, but right now, it is very difficult for me to listen. In this passage, the word "listen" appears eight times. For my dad, who'd never been exposed to the idea that wives needed to be listened to, this was revolutionary.
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>书里提供了简单的建议，比如，当男人学会倾听和解读女人的感受时，沟通就会变得更容易。如果他在倾听时感到非常沮丧，他不应该试图继续。相反，尊重的方式是说：我真的很想听你在说什么，但现在，我很难听进去。在这段话中，“倾听”这个词出现了八次。对于我父亲这种从来没接触过“妻子需要被倾听”这个想法的人来说，这是革命性的。

00:16:54.720 --> 00:17:06.070
<v Abbas Rafsanjani>You know, I thought I'm right, that because I'm right, now everybody is not--
<v Abbas Rafsanjani>你知道，我以为我是对的，因为我是对的，现在每个人都不……

00:17:06.070 --> 00:17:06.460
<v Mina Rafsanjani>[INAUDIBLE]
<v Mina Rafsanjani>[听不清]

00:17:06.460 --> 00:17:19.440
<v Abbas Rafsanjani>Yeah, because of that. And some chapter of this book is give me, so OK, no, you wasn't right. You have to relax and then listen.
<v Abbas Rafsanjani>是的，因为那个。这本书的一些章节让我意识到，好吧，不，你不对。你得放松，然后倾听。

00:17:19.440 --> 00:17:33.660
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>My dad started dating. But the book didn't teach him not to talk about his ex-wife on his dates, so nothing was working out. But he was determined not to be alone. So he had relatives in Iran introduce him to a woman, and he got engaged.
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>我父亲开始约会。但这本书没教他不要在约会时谈论前妻，所以什么都不顺利。但他决心不再一个人生活。于是他让伊朗的亲戚介绍了一位女士，他订婚了。

00:17:33.660 --> 00:17:56.520
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>By this time, about 2 and 1/2 years after their divorce, I was more concerned about my mother than my father. It was my father who, of the two of them, was seeming more liberated. My mother just seemed stuck. Every time I went to her house, there were three maps of San Jose covering her kitchen table. She'd get home from work and just sit and stare at them, tracing out new and better routes she could take to her office.
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>到这个时候，在他们离婚两年半后，比起父亲，我更担心我母亲。他们两人中，看起来更解放的人是我父亲。我母亲只是显得停滞不前。每次我去她家，她的餐桌上都铺着三张圣何塞的地图。她下班回家，就坐在那儿盯着它们，描绘出她去办公室的更好、更新的路线。

00:17:56.520 --> 00:18:07.880
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>All of her willpower had been used up just getting herself free. I remember being so disappointed in my mom. She'd come this far, and then sort of just stopped. There was nothing left in her to find someone new.
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>她所有的意志力都耗费在让自己获得自由上了。我记得对母亲感到非常失望。她走了这么远，然后就停下了。她身上再也没有动力去寻找新人了。

00:18:07.880 --> 00:18:22.940
<v Mina Rafsanjani>In one point, I was in an airplane one day, and I saw a couple of old couples. And I thought, oh, my god, I wish I was them. I wish I had my, like, 30-years husband beside me.
<v Mina Rafsanjani>有一次，我在飞机上，我看到了一对老夫妇。我想，哦，天哪，我希望我是他们。我希望我有像我那陪伴了30年的丈夫在我身边。

00:18:22.940 --> 00:18:49.370
<v Mina Rafsanjani>And I was sitting behind them, and she started to explain to the guy that this is her friends, and this is that, and this is-- I thought oh, they're dating. They're not husband and wife. I thought, I don't want to-- in age 50, I don't want to explain to somebody my every single detail of my life. I want somebody to know me.
<v Mina Rafsanjani>我坐在他们后面，她开始向那个人解释这是她的朋友，那是那个……我想，哦，他们在约会。他们不是夫妻。我想，我不想——在50岁的时候，我不想向某人解释我生活中的每一个细节。我想要一个了解我的人。

00:18:49.370 --> 00:19:07.150
<v Mina Rafsanjani>And yes, he gets angry. Yes, he's short-tempered. Yes, he's that, but I got old with him.
<v Mina Rafsanjani>是的，他会生气。是的，他脾气暴躁。是的，他是那样，但我和他一起变老了。

00:19:07.150 --> 00:19:20.280
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>Not long after that came the day my mom did the second most surprising thing she would ever do in her life. It was two days after her 49th birthday. She called my sister to get together and celebrate, but my sister was busy.
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>没过多久，我母亲就做了她一生中最令人惊讶的第二件事。那是她49岁生日后的两天。她打电话给我妹妹想聚在一起庆祝，但我妹妹很忙。

00:19:20.280 --> 00:19:46.250
<v Mina Rafsanjani>She said, no, we celebrated your birthday year-- I mean, a week after we were going somewhere. And why don't you get a boyfriend? Nah, dah, dah, dah, dah. [LAUGHS] She said, if you're lonely, you have to get a boyfriend, and your problem is going to be solved. You know, we have a life. And that day I thought, this is not working. I need to fix this.
<v Mina Rafsanjani>她说，不，我们庆祝了你的生日……我是说，一周后我们要去别的地方。你为什么不找个男朋友？[笑] 她说，如果你寂寞，就得找个男朋友，你的问题就解决了。你知道，我们有自己的生活。那天我想，这样不行。我得解决这个问题。

00:19:46.250 --> 00:20:03.810
<v Mina Rafsanjani>This is not working. I have to leave these kids alone. I have to get somewhere that I don't need her that much. I thought, let me try that old man again. [LAUGHS]
<v Mina Rafsanjani>这样不行。我得让孩子们独自生活。我得去一个我不需要那么依赖她的地方。我想，让我再试试那个老头子吧。[笑]

00:20:03.810 --> 00:20:17.720
<v Abbas Rafsanjani>One day, I remember Sunday afternoon, telephone is ringing. And I pick up the phone. After, I think, one year, it is Mina.
<v Abbas Rafsanjani>有一天，我记得是星期天下午，电话响了。我接了电话。我想，一年后，是米娜。

00:20:17.720 --> 00:20:34.130
<v Mina Rafsanjani>I called him, and he got so surprised. He says, what's going on? Are you OK? I said, yeah, I'm OK. I was wondering, when is your wedding day? He said, well, two months from now. And you know, it's just I'm flying two weeks from now. I said, well, congratulations.
<v Mina Rafsanjani>我打给他，他很惊讶。他说，怎么了？你还好吗？我说，是啊，我很好。我想知道，你的结婚日是什么时候？他说，好吧，两个月后。你知道，我两周后就要飞过去了。我说，好吧，恭喜你。

00:20:34.130 --> 00:20:47.050
<v Abbas Rafsanjani>I told you, I am going to get married with this lady. Even I told you everything. Do you want to come back? Still, I am coming. I want to come back to you.
<v Abbas Rafsanjani>我告诉过你，我要和这位女士结婚。甚至我把一切都告诉你了。你想回来吗？我还想回来。我想回到你身边。

00:20:47.050 --> 00:20:55.070
<v Mina Rafsanjani>You know, I said, you're getting married, for god sake. He said, well, yeah, but actually, you are my wife.
<v Mina Rafsanjani>你知道，我说，你都要结婚了，天哪。他说，好吧，是的，但实际上，你才是我的妻子。

00:20:55.070 --> 00:21:08.090
<v Abbas Rafsanjani>Suddenly, she started crying, very bad. I was worried about her. I was very, very upset again. I didn't know what should I do now.
<v Abbas Rafsanjani>突然，她开始哭得很厉害。我担心她。我又非常非常难过。我不知道我现在该做什么。

00:21:08.090 --> 00:21:21.810
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>My dad was two weeks away from going to collect his new bride. He felt awful about standing her up, but he'd never stopped loving my mom, even though he'd spent the last two years trying to forget about her. And so he didn't really have a choice.
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>我父亲离去迎娶新娘还有两周。他对自己要爽约感到非常糟糕，但他从未停止爱我母亲，尽管他花了过去两年试图忘记她。所以他真的别无选择。

00:21:21.810 --> 00:21:34.440
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>My dad promised my mom that he'd changed, but she didn't want to move too fast. So my parents, for the first time in their lives ever, started dating-- well, sort of. On their first date, they didn't leave the house.
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>我父亲向我母亲保证他已经改变了，但她不想发展得太快。所以我的父母，一生中第一次，开始约会了——嗯，差不多吧。在他们的第一次约会上，他们没有离开家。

00:21:34.440 --> 00:21:46.370
<v Mina Rafsanjani>He came to my house. And you know, we talked. And in fact, I cried all throughout.
<v Mina Rafsanjani>他来到我家。你知道，我们谈了谈。事实上，我哭了一整晚。

00:21:46.370 --> 00:21:56.800
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>Why are you crying now? Do you know? Is it because-- have you not talked-- is it because you haven't talked about this for a while?
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>你为什么现在在哭？你知道吗？是因为——你还没谈过——是因为你有一段时间没谈论这个了吗？

00:21:56.800 --> 00:22:37.080
<v Mina Rafsanjani>Well, I think, when I look back, whole thing was a sad event. And you know, I just told him, you know, what have we done regarding that point? He said, well, I did tons of mistakes, but you were with me with all those mistakes. Because you should have, you know been firmer. You should have let me know that how much you hate me.
<v Mina Rafsanjani>嗯，我想，当我回首往事，整个事件是一次令人悲伤的经历。你知道，我只是告诉他，关于那一点我们做了什么？他说，嗯，我犯了很多错误，但你一直陪伴着我，包容那些错误。因为你应该，你知道，更坚定一点。你应该让我知道你有多讨厌我。

00:22:37.080 --> 00:23:07.980
<v Mina Rafsanjani>You never told me that you hate me. You never told me that you were that much upset. You always said, yes, yes, yes, yes, and-- you know? And he listened. You know, I told him everything, and he never got upset, or never got offended, or never got red and blue, or-- you know?
<v Mina Rafsanjani>你从来没告诉过我你讨厌我。你从来没告诉过我你那么难过。你总是说，是，是，是，是，然后——你知道吗？他倾听了。你知道，我告诉了他一切，他从来没有难过，从来没有感到被冒犯，从来没有气得脸红脖子粗——你知道吗？

00:23:07.980 --> 00:23:41.430
<v Mina Rafsanjani>So I told him everything that I was upset about that I never had the guts to tell him. And he listened. And he said, you're absolutely right. I lost you. You were the most precious thing that I ever got. And so I want to fix that, if you let me.
<v Mina Rafsanjani>所以我告诉了他我感到难过的所有事情，我以前没有勇气告诉他的事情。他倾听了。他说，你是绝对正确的。我失去了你。你是我得到的最宝贵的东西。所以我想弥补这一点，如果你允许的话。

00:23:41.430 --> 00:23:56.550
<v Abbas Rafsanjani>It was kind of relief, yeah. We had a lot of information. We have to release for 25 years' marriage what parts we did wrong and what part it was OK.
<v Abbas Rafsanjani>这是一种解脱，是的。我们获得了很多信息。我们必须释放25年婚姻中我们做错的地方，以及哪些部分是还可以的。

00:23:56.550 --> 00:24:27.980
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>After our conversation and after he went to leave, he hugged me, and he said, you know, just let's try one more time, and let's see how it's going to go. And he hugged me, and I thought, oh, my god, feels good. [LAUGHS] I thought I fall into a shoulder that I can rely.
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>谈话结束后，在他离开之前，他拥抱了我，他说，你知道，咱们再试一次，看看结果会怎样。他拥抱了我，我想，哦，天哪，感觉真好。[笑] 我想我落入了一个我可以依靠的肩膀。

00:24:27.980 --> 00:24:48.930
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>The second courtship took six months, instead of three weeks. They got married at City Hall. Everyone they knew was overjoyed, except for my sister and me. We boycotted the wedding. It seemed ridiculous to us. After three years of worrying about them and holding their hands, they were heading straight back into the misery they'd worked so hard to leave behind.
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>第二次求爱持续了六个月，而不是三周。他们在市政厅结了婚。所有认识他们的人都欣喜若狂，除了我和我妹妹。我们抵制了婚礼。对我们来说这看起来太荒谬了。在为他们担心了三年并牵着他们的手之后，他们又径直走回了他们努力想离开的痛苦之中。

00:24:48.930 --> 00:25:10.490
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>But it turns out we were wrong. They traded their unhappy Iranian marriage for a happy American one. When you think about American relationships, you think about the divorces, the self-help books, the constant talking about feelings. And even though all of that seems undignified and laughable, it's all working for my parents.
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>但事实证明我们错了。他们用不幸的伊朗婚姻换取了幸福的美国式婚姻。当你想到美国式的关系时，你会想到离婚、自助书籍、不断谈论感受。即使这一切看起来既不庄重又可笑，但对我的父母来说都奏效了。

00:25:10.490 --> 00:25:21.550
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>Now I can see my dad struggling to keep his temper in check. He thinks about hurting my mom now, about embarrassing her in a way he never did before. I see them laugh with each other and talk to each other.
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>现在我可以看到父亲在努力控制脾气。他现在会考虑伤害母亲的事，考虑以一种他以前从未有过的方式让她尴尬。我看到他们互相交谈、开怀大笑。

00:25:21.550 --> 00:25:36.500
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>I used to be so nervous around them, because the atmosphere was so tense. Now, they actually seem to like each other. And for a long time, I attributed everything to my father's change. He learned to control his temper, and now they're happy. But that's not the way they see it.
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>我以前在他们身边很紧张，因为气氛太紧张了。现在，他们似乎真的喜欢对方。很长一段时间里，我把一切归功于父亲的改变。他学会了控制脾气，现在他们幸福了。但这并不是他们的看法。

00:25:36.500 --> 00:25:43.290
<v Abbas Rafsanjani>Divorce, I think, is changed Mina more than me also, a lot.
<v Abbas Rafsanjani>我想，离婚改变米娜比改变我也多得多。

00:25:43.290 --> 00:26:04.940
<v Mina Rafsanjani>Basically, I had a lot of problem in my life that he wasn't the cause of it. When two years I sat down, I always thought, he's not here. I still have those problems, you know? I don't socialize not because of-- it's not him, it's me.
<v Mina Rafsanjani>基本上，我生活中有很多问题不是他造成的。当我坐下来那两年，我总是想，他不在了。我仍然有那些问题，你知道？我不社交不是因为……不是他，是我自己。

00:26:04.940 --> 00:26:22.660
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>Living on her own allowed my mother to see my father the way he'd always seen her-- as a choice she wanted to make. And now she says she loves him for the first time in her life.
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>独自生活让我母亲能够用他一直看待她的眼光看待我父亲——作为她想要做出的一个选择。现在她说她一生中第一次爱上了他。

00:26:22.660 --> 00:26:32.020
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>Everybody agrees that if they'd stayed in Iran, none of this would have happened-- not the heartbreak, not the new-found compatibility. This is my sister, Nilofar.
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>大家都同意，如果他们留在伊朗，这一切都不会发生——没有心碎，也没有新发现的契合度。这是我的妹妹，尼洛法。

00:26:32.020 --> 00:26:58.380
<v Nilofar Rafsanjani>The West did break them up. And then, they had the freedom to get back together again, you know? I always felt like we had such a weirdly and kind of such an American experience, like, all of a sudden in the middle. And I don't know what even that means except that my parents got divorced, and then they talked about their feelings, and they both went to therapy. And my dad read Men are From Mars, Women are From Venus.
<v Nilofar Rafsanjani>西方确实让他们分开了。然后，他们又有了重新在一起的自由，你知道吗？我一直觉得我们的经历很奇怪，甚至有点美国式的，就像中间突然出现的那样。我不知道那意味着什么，只知道我的父母离婚了，然后他们谈论了各自的感受，他们俩都去接受了治疗。而且我父亲读了《男人来自火星，女人来自金星》。

00:26:58.380 --> 00:26:58.720
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>He did, among other things.
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>他确实读了，还有其他书。

00:26:58.720 --> 00:27:12.820
<v Nilofar Rafsanjani>And we talked to my dad about relationships. And he started dating. And I was just like-- it's like I feel like we had this family, like the crazy family on the block who doesn't put up Christmas lights, like the non-American, crazy family.
<v Nilofar Rafsanjani>我们和父亲谈论过人际关系。他开始约会。我就觉得——我觉得我们就像那个社区里古怪的家庭，那种不挂圣诞彩灯的，不那么美国化的、疯狂的家庭。

00:27:12.820 --> 00:27:13.120
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>With a bad lawn.
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>还有杂草丛生的草坪。

00:27:13.120 --> 00:27:31.000
<v Nilofar Rafsanjani>With a bad lawn all of our lives. And then all of a sudden, my parents turned All-American and had this American experience. And now we're like this little happy family. And it kind of grosses me out too, because I'm like, this is not where we fit. You know? This is not our family. You know what I mean?
<v Nilofar Rafsanjani>一辈子都有糟糕的草坪。然后突然之间，我的父母变成了地道的美国人，有了这种美国式的体验。现在我们就像这个小小的幸福家庭。这甚至让我觉得恶心，因为我就想，这不适合我们。你知道吗？这不是我们的家庭。你明白我的意思吗？

00:27:31.000 --> 00:27:31.730
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>I love it.
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>我喜欢。

00:27:31.730 --> 00:27:38.530
<v Nilofar Rafsanjani>I think it's great. I love it. It feels really normal and good now.
<v Nilofar Rafsanjani>我觉得这很好。我喜欢它。现在感觉真的很正常，很好。

00:27:38.530 --> 00:27:51.400
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>I'm still struggling with normal and good. When you thought about your life as some slightly depressing, slightly boring novel lying on the bargain bookshelf, it's weird to have it end up on the bestseller rack in the self-help section.
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>我还在努力适应“正常和美好”。当你把生活想成那种放在廉价书架上有点压抑、有点无聊的小说时，结局最终出现在自助类书籍的畅销书架上是很奇怪的。

