<v Delilah Ashford Potts>[公牛吼叫]

00:32:20.780 --> 00:32:46.970
<v Ira Glass>So Sandra and Ralph had this really sweet situation. They got to work side-by-side with all these animals, which they loved. Chance was so tame that he was allowed out of the pasture and even had a favorite spot in the front yard, under the trees. Sandra would watch him from the kitchen window, which was a really nice feeling. You'd be doing the dishes, and you'd look out, and there's your tame pet bull, like Ferdinand, laying on the grass.
<v Ira Glass>所以桑德拉和拉尔夫有这种非常甜蜜的情况。他们可以和他们热爱的所有动物并肩工作。它温顺到被允许离开牧场，甚至在院子里的树下有自己最喜欢的休息点。桑德拉会从厨房窗户看着它，那感觉真的很好。你在洗碗，往外看，那是你的温顺宠物公牛，像斐迪南一样躺在草地上。

00:32:46.970 --> 00:32:59.610
<v Ira Glass>Then, a rainy day in September, Chance didn't come in from the pasture to eat. Sandra went looking for him. And finally, Ralph headed out to the pasture and found him dead. He was 19, which was really old for a bull.
<v Ira Glass>然后，九月的一个雨天，“机会”没有从牧场回来吃东西。桑德拉去寻找它。最后，拉尔夫前往牧场发现了它已经死了。它19岁了，对公牛来说真的非常老了。

00:32:59.610 --> 00:33:14.210
<v Ralph Fisher>It was a really a sad day. I took the camera out, and I was going to take some pictures. And then I said, well, you know, he deserves more than that. So I skinned him. I skinned his whole body. It took me all day. It was raining.
<v Ralph Fisher>那真是悲伤的一天。我拿出相机，想拍些照片。然后我说，嗯，你知道，它值得比这更多。所以我剥了它的皮。我剥了它全身的皮。花了我一整天。当时正在下雨。

00:33:14.210 --> 00:33:36.300
<v Ralph Fisher>So it was a really sad time. I'd skin a while, and cry a while. I was just like a baby, just crying like a baby out there with my knife, you know, skinning him. And I'm going, well, somebody has to do this. And I'd have to toughen up and regroup. And this lasted all day long. And I guess I was a complete wreck by the time it was all over with
<v Ralph Fisher>所以那是一段非常悲伤的时光。我剥一会儿皮，哭一会儿。我就像个婴儿，在外面拿着刀哭得像个婴儿，你知道，剥着它的皮。我心想，好吧，总得有人做这件事。我得坚强起来，重整旗鼓。这持续了一整天。我猜当这一切结束时，我彻底崩溃了。

00:33:36.300 --> 00:33:50.550
<v Ira Glass>Ralph says he had no choice. He had to skin him in the first 24 hours, because he wanted to get him taxidermied, turn him into a full-sized Brahman statue, have been around forever. And people could still have their picture taken with him. Chance could stay in the family business.
<v Ira Glass>拉尔夫说他别无选择。他必须在24小时内剥皮，因为他想把它做成标本，把它变成一尊全尺寸的婆罗门公牛雕像，让它永远存在。这样人们仍然可以和它合影。“机会”可以留在家族企业里。

00:33:50.550 --> 00:34:05.460
<v Ralph Fisher>So I was so emotional-- everything was really emotional. And all the family had a good cry too-- all of my kids and the people who knew him so long. They all just hated that.
<v Ralph Fisher>所以我太情绪化了——一切都真的很感性。所有的家人也都痛哭了一场——我所有的孩子和认识它很久的人。他们都讨厌这样。

00:34:05.460 --> 00:34:19.900
<v Ira Glass>OK, rewind. Three months before all that happened, Ralph heard about this way that he might be able to cheat death with Chance and keep him around. It was more than taxidermy, but it was a real long shot.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，倒带。在这所有事情发生前三个月，拉尔夫听说了一种他可能能够用“机会”欺骗死亡并将它留住的方法。那不仅仅是标本制作，但这是一个真正渺茫的机会。

00:34:19.900 --> 00:34:33.940
<v Ralph Fisher>Someone called me and said, we've read an article in the paper that A&M is going to clone an animal. What do you mean, "clone?" They said, you know, clone, make one from another one. I said, oh, no, it can't be.
<v Ralph Fisher>有人打电话给我说，我们读了一篇报纸文章，说德州农工大学要克隆一只动物。你说的“克隆”是什么意思？他们说，你知道，克隆，制造一个和另一个一样的。我说，哦，不，不可能。

00:34:33.940 --> 00:34:42.650
<v Ira Glass>A&M is Texas A&M, a world-famous veterinary school and animal hospital. It just happens to be an hour and a half drive from Ralph's house. It was Chance's regular hospital.
<v Ira Glass>德州农工大学是一所世界著名的兽医学校和动物医院。它刚好距离拉尔夫家有1.5小时的车程。那是“机会”经常去的医院。

00:34:42.650 --> 00:35:02.740
<v Ralph Fisher>And so we called up and said, you know, you're going to clone an animal, right? They said, yes, we take tissue and we-- and we said, well, why don't you use Chance? And so other folks would call, then. Some friends would call and talk to him. And they finally just-- I think we bothered them so much, they gave up. They said, OK, well, we'll do it.
<v Ralph Fisher>于是我们打电话说，你知道，你们要克隆一只动物，对吧？他们说，是的，我们取组织，然后……我们说，好吧，你们为什么不用“机会”呢？其他亲友也打电话过来。他们最终……我想我们骚扰得他们实在受不了了，他们放弃了。他们说，好吧，我们会做的。

00:35:02.740 --> 00:35:24.470
<v Ira Glass>In the end, the scientists decided to clone Chance for a simple reason-- he was old. There were some things they thought they might find out by cloning an older animal. And Chance was there anyway, getting a mole biopsied for disease. The scientists could use the DNA from the mole to do their cloning.
<v Ira Glass>最后，科学家们决定克隆“机会”，原因很简单——它很老。他们认为通过克隆一只年长的动物可能会发现一些事情。而且“机会”当时刚好在那儿，正在为一个患病的痣做活检。科学家可以使用痣中的DNA进行克隆。

00:35:24.470 --> 00:35:29.190
<v News Announcer>In Texas tonight, a man, his wife, and their bull have been reunited.
<v News Announcer>今晚在德克萨斯，一个男人、他的妻子和他们的公牛重聚了。

00:35:29.190 --> 00:35:31.970
<v Ralph Fisher>Yeah, I guess he's been reincarnated.
<v Ralph Fisher>是的，我猜它已经轮回转世了。

00:35:31.970 --> 00:35:42.710
<v Ira Glass>10 months and 18 days after Chance died, the long shot paid off. A clone was born-- the first cloned bull. It was huge news. And it was Ralph who figured out what to call Chance's clone.
<v Ira Glass>“机会”死后10个月18天，这个渺茫的机会成功了。一个克隆体出生了——第一头克隆公牛。这是一个巨大的新闻。是拉尔夫想出了给这个克隆体叫什么名字。

00:35:42.710 --> 00:35:55.940
<v Ralph Fisher>Second Chance. And he was just the prettiest little white Brahman calf you ever saw. And spindly. And barely able to stand.
<v Ralph Fisher>“第二次机会”。它是你见过的最可爱的婆罗门小牛犊。瘦骨嶙峋。几乎站不稳。

00:35:55.940 --> 00:36:11.780
<v Ralph Fisher>I thought it was the same animal. I would say, you know, we got him back. That's the first headline, I think, in the news clippings when they interviewed me-- we got him back.
<v Ralph Fisher>我认为它就是同一只动物。我想，你知道，我们把它找回来了。我想，当他们采访我时，那是新闻剪报里的第一个标题——我们找回它了。

00:36:11.780 --> 00:36:26.100
<v Sandra Fisher>The vets at A&M would ask us, or kidded with us about whether the calf was going to recognize his own stall in the barn when we got him home. But we sort of blew that off.
<v Sandra Fisher>德州农工大学的兽医会问我们，或者开玩笑地问我们，当我们把它带回家时，它是否会认出它在谷仓里的隔间。但我们没当回事。

00:36:26.100 --> 00:36:46.390
<v Ralph Fisher>Well, the first day we brought him home, we turned him loose over here in the yard. And he went over and laid down in the same spot. This still kind of chokes me up. He laid down in the same spot that Chance laid in, you know? That's not reasonable.
<v Ralph Fisher>嗯，第一天我们把它带回家，我们在院子里把它放开。它走过去躺在同一个位置。这仍然让我有点哽咽。它躺在“机会”躺过的同一个位置，你知道吗？这不合理。

00:36:46.390 --> 00:36:51.350
<v Sandra Fisher>And it all was spooky. That was spooky.
<v Sandra Fisher>这一切都很诡异。这很诡异。

00:36:51.350 --> 00:37:06.080
<v Ira Glass>The other thing they noticed right away with Second Chances is that he has Chance's same mannerisms when he eats-- unusual mannerisms. Instead of putting his head into a feed bucket and holding it down there and chewing until there's no more food, both Chance and Second Chance raise their heads, close their eyes, and chew.
<v Ira Glass>他们注意到的关于“第二次机会”的另一件事是，它有和“机会”吃东西时一样的习惯——不同寻常的习惯。它们不是把头伸进饲料桶，一直低头嚼到没有食物为止，相反，“机会”和“第二次机会”都会抬起头，闭上眼睛，慢慢咀嚼。

00:37:06.080 --> 00:37:12.360
<v Ralph Fisher>I've never seen another animal do that. I've never seen another animal do that.
<v Ralph Fisher>我从没见过其他动物那样做。我从没见过其他动物那样做。

00:37:12.360 --> 00:37:16.460
<v Ira Glass>And there's something else about Second Chance, something harder to quantify.
<v Ira Glass>“第二次机会”还有别的东西，更难量化。

00:37:16.460 --> 00:37:39.830
<v Sandra Fisher>I just feel like he always recognized Ralph Fisher. And Ralph would lay down on the ground, and Second Chance would lick him and lick his boots. And Second Chance would lope across the pasture and come to us. And we watched other people. Vets would crawl in there, and he wouldn't come across the pasture to them. He just always seemed to recognize Ralph.
<v Sandra Fisher>我只是觉得它总是认出拉尔夫·费舍尔。拉尔夫会躺在地上，“第二次机会”会舔他，舔他的靴子。“第二次机会”会穿过牧场向我们奔来。我们观察其他人。兽医会爬进去，它不会穿过牧场走向他们。它似乎总是认出拉尔夫。

00:37:39.830 --> 00:37:43.160
<v Ira Glass>And what did you think? Did you think somehow his memory got transferred?
<v Ira Glass>你当时怎么想？你觉得某种方式上它的记忆转移了吗？

00:37:43.160 --> 00:38:00.570
<v Sandra Fisher>Well, no. I still don't think that. I think that he just has the same instincts, the same-- that he's going to make the same choices. Why would the eating thing be the same?
<v Sandra Fisher>嗯，没有。我仍然不这么认为。我觉得它只是有同样的本能，同样的……它会做出同样的选择。为什么吃东西的习惯会一样？

00:38:00.570 --> 00:38:06.770
<v Mark Westhusin>A clone is akin to a genetically identical twin. It's not the same animal. It's not Chance.
<v Mark Westhusin>克隆体类似于基因完全相同的双胞胎。它不是同一只动物。它不是“机会”。

00:38:06.770 --> 00:38:26.020
<v Ira Glass>Dr. Mark Westhusin is one of the scientists who runs the Reproductive Sciences Lab at Texas A&M, the lab that cloned Second Chance. And he says that because a clone is just a twin-- nothing more, nothing less-- it may not even look the same as the original. And just like twins, clones don't necessarily act like the originals either. But Dr. Westhusin understands how Ralph or Sandra may not see it that way.
<v Ira Glass>马克·韦斯胡森博士是运营德州农工大学生殖科学实验室的科学家之一，就是那个克隆了“第二次机会”的实验室。他说，因为克隆体只是一个双胞胎——不多不少——它甚至看起来可能和原件不一样。而且就像双胞胎一样，克隆体也不一定像原型那样行事。但韦斯胡森博士理解为什么拉尔夫或桑德拉可能不这么看。

00:38:26.020 --> 00:38:43.960
<v Mark Westhusin>Well, people get attached to their animals, and they-- you know? And they want to, sometimes, see more than is there. And they do see more than is, maybe, really there. And that's not necessarily a bad thing. I mean, that's like--
<v Mark Westhusin>嗯，人们对他们的动物有感情，而且他们……你知道吗？他们有时想看到比实际存在更多的东西。他们确实看到了比实际存在的更多的东西。这不一定是坏事。我是说，这就像……

00:38:43.960 --> 00:38:44.980
<v Ira Glass>That's love, right there.
<v Ira Glass>那就是爱，对吧？

00:38:44.980 --> 00:39:05.950
<v Mark Westhusin>Yeah, that's love. That's not necessarily a bad thing. And it was interesting for me to listen to Ralph tell how he lies under the same tree, and he eats his food just the same as the old Chance did. And I'm like, that's really cool, Ralph, you know? But I just-- how's his blood glucose today? [LAUGHS] I'm interested in that, you know?
<v Mark Westhusin>是的，那是爱。这不一定是坏事。听拉尔夫说它如何躺在同一棵树下，吃食物的方式和老“机会”一模一样，这对我来说很有趣。我心想，这真的太酷了，拉尔夫。但我只是……它今天的血糖怎么样？[笑] 我对那个感兴趣，你知道吗？

00:39:05.950 --> 00:39:33.610
<v Ralph Fisher>Dr. Westhusin reminded me several times, this is not the same animal. Don't expect the same attitude from a pet. And I didn't believe him, because everything looked the same. Just when I see him, especially at a distance, he looks so much like Chance. And every day, as he gets older and older, so much.
<v Ralph Fisher>韦斯胡森博士提醒过我几次，这不是同一只动物。不要指望宠物有同样的态度。我当时不相信他，因为一切看起来都一样。当我看到它时，尤其是在远处，它看起来太像“机会”了。而且每天，随着它越来越大，越来越像。

00:39:33.610 --> 00:39:50.110
<v Ira Glass>Every year on Second Chance's birthday, Ralph and Sandra would throw a party for him, fire up some barbecue-- which, if you think about it, would be so disturbing for the guest of honor, if only he knew. Anyway, on his fourth birthday, all the family, and friends, and neighbors came to the front yard, posed for pictures with Second Chance.
<v Ira Glass>在“第二次机会”生日那天，拉尔夫和桑德拉会为它举办派对，烤些烧烤——如果你想一想，如果被邀请的嘉宾知道真相，这会让人多么不安。总之，在它四岁生日时，所有的家人、朋友和邻居都来到前院，和“第二次机会”合影。

00:39:50.110 --> 00:40:05.360
<v Ralph Fisher>We had his birthday cake, which is a big feed tub with candles on top. And we blew the candles out. And I said, it's about dark, so I think I'll lead him back to the barn.
<v Ralph Fisher>我们有它的生日蛋糕，那是一个放着蜡烛的大饲料桶。我们吹灭了蜡烛。我说，天快黑了，我想我带它回谷仓。

00:40:05.360 --> 00:40:16.650
<v Ralph Fisher>I put one finger in his nose ring, which is a common thing. It gives you close contact. And if they make any aggression, you can immediately feel it and respond, you know?
<v Ralph Fisher>我把一根手指塞进它的鼻环里，这是一种常见的做法。这能让你有近距离接触。如果它们有任何攻击倾向，你可以立即感觉到并做出反应，你知道吗？

00:40:16.650 --> 00:40:40.840
<v Ralph Fisher>Anyway, I was walking him. We just did a few steps out of the yard, and I had the lead rope in the other hand. We just took a few steps, and then, boom, caught me with his horn and slammed me down. And that's when he dislocated my shoulder. Hit me in the back, so I was immobile on that side. He picked me up and [MAKES WHOOSH NOISE] slammed me down, you see?
<v Ralph Fisher>总之，我在牵着它走。我们刚走出院子几步，我另一只手拿着牵引绳。我们刚走了几步，然后，砰，它用角顶住我，把我猛摔在地。那就是它让我的肩膀脱臼的时候。撞在我的背上，所以我那一侧动弹不得。它把我挑起来，[发出嗖的一声]猛摔下去，你明白吗？

00:40:40.840 --> 00:41:02.560
<v Ralph Fisher>And then he proceeded to get on top of me with mostly his head, his forehead, and his horns. We found three deep, deep holes, six or eight inches, in the yard that next day where he had missed me. And he was really trying-- he was really attacking. It was like, you know, on purpose.
<v Ralph Fisher>然后它开始用它的头部，主要是前额和角骑在我身上。第二天，我们在院子里发现了三个很深很深的洞，六到八英寸，它本来应该把我戳穿的。它真的是在尝试——它真的是在攻击。就好像，你知道，它是故意的。

00:41:02.560 --> 00:41:33.620
<v Ralph Fisher>Oh, no. I said, oh no, this can't be. This can't be happening. Because we had had so much faith in him. And you know, oh, it just-- you know, I didn't-- at that time, all I was thinking about was, why are you doing this? Why? And I was just so disappointed the whole time. I remember, when I was kicking him in the face, I was just going, how could you be doing this to me? Quit it. I think my feelings-- he actually hurt my pride.
<v Ralph Fisher>哦，不。我说，哦，不，这不可能。这不可能发生。因为我们对它有太多的信任。你知道，这真的……那时，我满脑子想的只是，你为什么这样做？为什么？我一直感到非常失望。我记得当我踢它的脸时，我只是在想，你怎么能对我做这种事？住手。我想我的感受——它确实伤害了我的自尊。

00:41:33.620 --> 00:41:41.060
<v Ira Glass>Since the attack, Ralph acknowledges Second Chance is not the same animal as Chance. And he believes it. He definitely believes it.
<v Ira Glass>自那次袭击以来，拉尔夫承认“第二次机会”和“机会”不是同一只动物。他相信了。他绝对相信了。

00:41:41.060 --> 00:41:54.520
<v Ira Glass>When you love, you see what you want to see. Everybody knows that. And part of him still holds out hope-- hope that even if Second Chance is not Chance right now, he'll turn into Chance, they'll still get their reunion.
<v Ira Glass>当你去爱的时候，你只看到你想看到的东西。每个人都知道这一点。他内心深处仍然抱有希望——希望即使“第二次机会”现在不是“机会”，它也会变成“机会”，他们仍然能实现重聚。

00:41:54.520 --> 00:42:02.620
<v Ralph Fisher>Well, the hope is involving his age, since I did not have chance until he was almost seven years old.
<v Ralph Fisher>嗯，希望与它的年龄有关，因为直到它快七岁时我才拥有“机会”。

00:42:02.620 --> 00:42:03.360
<v Ira Glass>Right.
<v Ira Glass>对。

00:42:03.360 --> 00:42:07.550
<v Ralph Fisher>I don't really know how Second Chance is supposed to act.
<v Ralph Fisher>我真的不知道“第二次机会”应该怎么表现。

00:42:07.550 --> 00:42:10.220
<v Ira Glass>Oh, you don't know if Chance was just like this back in the day?
<v Ira Glass>哦，你不知道“机会”以前是不是也这样？

00:42:10.220 --> 00:42:20.340
<v Ralph Fisher>Right. Right. So he might just have to go through a little stage and settle down. I know horses settle down. My Texas Longhorns settle down.
<v Ralph Fisher>对。对。所以它可能只是要经历一个小的阶段然后沉淀下来。我知道马会沉淀下来。我的德克萨斯长角牛也会沉淀下来。

00:42:20.340 --> 00:42:41.810
<v Ira Glass>Ralph, here's one of things that I've been really wondering about your situation. I wonder if having Second Chance around all the time is ever painful for you, because it reminds you that you don't have Chance? It's like, here, you have this ghost of your favorite animal walking the property, who isn't exactly Chance, and if it just kind of breaks your heart a little bit?
<v Ira Glass>拉尔夫，关于你的情况，我一直很好奇一件事。我想知道身边一直有“第二次机会”是否让你感到痛苦，因为它提醒你你没有“机会”了？就像，这里有你最喜欢的动物的幽灵在你的地盘上走来走去，但它并不是真正的“机会”，这是否有点让你心碎？

00:42:41.810 --> 00:42:58.880
<v Ralph Fisher>No, sir, it's just the opposite. You're just exactly as wrong as you can be. So far, right now, I feel like we've gotten about 95% of him back-- I mean, the same qualities, the same fun. That satisfies me. That's better than zero.
<v Ralph Fisher>不，先生，恰恰相反。你错得离谱。到目前为止，我觉得我们已经找回了它95%的特质——我是说，同样的品质，同样的乐趣。这让我满足了。这总比零好。

00:42:58.880 --> 00:43:13.090
<v Ralph Fisher>When he was laying out in that pasture out there dead, he was a zero. I mean, we would never have any enjoyment out of him any more. And there's a tremendous difference between 0 and 95%.
<v Ralph Fisher>当它躺在那个牧场里死去时，它就是零。我是说，我们再也不会从它身上得到任何乐趣了。而在0和95%之间有巨大的差别。

00:43:13.090 --> 00:43:21.390
<v Mark Westhusin>People want to believe it is resurrection, sometimes. And it is, in fact, not resurrection. It's reproduction.
<v Mark Westhusin>人们有时希望相信这是复活。但实际上，这不是复活。这是复制。

00:43:21.390 --> 00:43:32.080
<v Ira Glass>This is bad news Dr. Westhusin has had to deliver to a lot of people over the years. It was his lab at Texas A&M that cloned the first house pet, that cloned a cat named CC. It was international news.
<v Ira Glass>这是韦斯胡森博士多年来不得不向许多人传达的坏消息。正是他在德州农工大学的实验室克隆了第一只家养宠物，克隆了一只叫“CC”的猫。那是国际新闻。

00:43:32.080 --> 00:43:46.390
<v Ira Glass>And for a while, his lab got a lot of money from investors who wanted to start a business cloning people's pets-- which they've gotten away from now. But for a while, the scientists were deluged with calls from pet owners, people who wanted what Ralph and Sandra wanted-- they wanted their animals back.
<v Ira Glass>有一段时间，他的实验室从投资者那里得到了很多钱，这些人想开办一家克隆人们宠物的企业——他们现在已经不做了。但有一段时间，科学家们被宠物主人打来的电话淹没了，这些人想要拉尔夫和桑德拉想要的东西——他们想要回他们的动物。

00:43:46.390 --> 00:44:11.700
<v Mark Westhusin>You know, some were like, I have hair in the jewelry box that I've kept for 10 years. And will that work? No. You know, I had several calls where the animals had already been buried, and they were so-- you know, I didn't think about this till after I buried him. Do you think there'd be any possibility we could go out in the yard and dig it up and get some tissues to clone this animal back?
<v Mark Westhusin>你知道，有些人说，我首饰盒里留着它10年前的毛发。那管用吗？不。你知道，我有几次接到电话，动物已经埋葬了，他们说……你知道，我埋了它之后才想到这一点。你认为我们有可能去院子里把它挖出来，拿点组织来克隆这个动物吗？

00:44:11.700 --> 00:44:31.120
<v Mark Westhusin>And I'm just like, well, no, I don't think so. It's going to be pretty ran and full of bacteria. And we probably will not be able to get the tissue. And they'd say, well, it's cold up here, you know? And it was frozen when we did it, so I'm sure the carcass is still frozen. And it hasn't deteriorated too much. What do you think?
<v Mark Westhusin>我只是说，嗯，不，我不这么认为。那会腐烂且充满细菌。我们可能无法获得组织。他们会说，嗯，这里很冷，你知道？我们做的时候它是冻住的，所以我确定尸体现在还是冻住的。它没有腐烂太多。你怎么看？

00:44:31.120 --> 00:44:46.630
<v Ira Glass>When I visited Ralph's ranch to interview him and Sandra, it had been about a year and a half since the birthday party where Second Chance attacked Ralph. There were a bunch of us on this trip, including a film crew. And one night while we were taping up by the house, we heard a scream. And one of the crew came running up from the barn where Ralph and Second Chance were.
<v Ira Glass>当我访问拉尔夫的牧场去采访他和桑德拉时，距离“第二次机会”攻击拉尔夫的生日派对已经过去了一年半了。我们此行有很多人，包括一个拍摄组。一天晚上，当我们在房子附近录像时，我们听到了一声尖叫。其中一个工作人员从拉尔夫和“第二次机会”所在的谷仓跑过来。

00:44:46.630 --> 00:44:46.990
<v Woman 2>[INAUDIBLE]?
<v Woman 2>[听不清]？

00:44:46.990 --> 00:44:48.480
<v Man 1>What?
<v Man 1>什么？

00:44:48.480 --> 00:44:49.080
<v Woman 2>You guys?
<v Woman 2>你们？

00:44:49.080 --> 00:44:49.870
<v Man 1>What?
<v Man 1>什么？

00:44:49.870 --> 00:44:52.390
<v Woman 2>[INAUDIBLE], the bull attacked him.
<v Woman 2>[听不清]，公牛攻击了他。

00:44:52.390 --> 00:44:52.780
<v Man 1>Who?
<v Man 1>谁？

00:44:52.780 --> 00:44:53.710
<v Ira Glass>Oh, my god.
<v Ira Glass>哦，天哪。

00:44:53.710 --> 00:44:58.650
<v Woman 2>The bull attacked him. What's the address here?
<v Woman 2>公牛攻击了他。这里的地址是什么？

00:44:58.650 --> 00:45:11.410
<v Ira Glass>We threw Ralph into a car and rushed him into town to the nearest hospital. He was banged up pretty badly. Finally, Sandra came out from the emergency room to tell us that he'd be getting 80 stitches where Second Chance gored him.
<v Ira Glass>我们把拉尔夫塞进车里，急忙把他送到镇上最近的医院。他受了重伤。最后，桑德拉从急诊室出来告诉我们，他缝了80针，是“第二次机会”顶伤的。

00:45:11.410 --> 00:45:14.020
<v Sandra Fisher>The doctor is examining him right now.
<v Sandra Fisher>医生正在给他检查。

00:45:14.020 --> 00:45:17.460
<v Man 1>Yeah. And he gored him, like, in his crotch?
<v Man 1>是的。它顶伤了他的，像，胯部？

00:45:17.460 --> 00:45:24.320
<v Sandra Fisher>It's his left scrotum is ripped, and his testicle's hanging out. [SIGHS]
<v Sandra Fisher>它的左侧阴囊被撕裂了，睾丸露出来了。[叹气]

00:45:24.320 --> 00:45:28.510
<v Man 1>Oh, god.
<v Man 1>哦，天哪。

00:45:28.510 --> 00:45:39.040
<v Ira Glass>One of the producers of This American Life, Jane Marie, was in the barn, and she saw the attack. She says Ralph was bringing Second Chance some feed, trying to back him into a pen, when Second Chances just tossed Ralph.
<v Ira Glass>《美国生活》的一位制片人简·玛丽当时在谷仓里，她目睹了这次袭击。她说，当时拉尔夫正给“第二次机会”拿饲料，试图把它赶回围栏，“第二次机会”就直接顶翻了拉尔夫。

00:45:39.040 --> 00:45:43.640
<v Jane Marie>I screamed. And I didn't want to look at it because I thought he was going to kill him, you know? And I didn't want--
<v Jane Marie>我尖叫起来。我不想看，因为我以为它会杀了他，你知道吗？我不想——

00:45:43.640 --> 00:45:44.800
<v Ira Glass>And what did you see?
<v Ira Glass>你看到了什么？

00:45:44.800 --> 00:45:59.210
<v Jane Marie>I just saw him fly up in the air, and then the bull-- it was loud. It seemed really loud, because it was at the corner of the fence. So he threw up in the air, and he landed on the corner of the fences. And he's ramming him in the ground, into the fence. So the fences are all shaking, and the bull's making all the noises.
<v Jane Marie>我只看到他飞到空中，然后那头公牛——声音很大。声音听起来真的很大，因为它是在栅栏的角落。所以他被顶到空中，落在栅栏的角落。它在地上撞他，撞向栅栏。栅栏都在震动，公牛发出各种声音。

00:45:59.210 --> 00:45:59.880
<v Woman 3>[INAUDIBLE]
<v Woman 3>[听不清]

00:45:59.880 --> 00:46:14.690
<v Jane Marie>And it seems like the bull's going to break through the fences after he's done killing him, you know what I mean? It was very, very loud, very, very fast.
<v Jane Marie>看起来那头公牛在杀了他之后还要冲破栅栏，你懂我的意思吗？声音非常非常大，非常非常快。

00:46:14.690 --> 00:46:39.400
<v Ira Glass>It's almost like Second Chance was trying to tell Ralph that he's not Chance, using the only language he has. And of course, he's not Chance. In movies and books, when somebody tries to bring back a loved one from the dead-- whether it's a pet, or if it's a person-- it never works out well. It's one of the all-time ranking bad ideas, right up there with deciding to give robots and computers human feelings, or going back in time to change just one little thing in the past.
<v Ira Glass>简直就像“第二次机会”在尝试用它唯一拥有的语言告诉拉尔夫，它不是“机会”。当然，它不是“机会”。在电影和书中，当有人试图带回已故的爱人时——无论是宠物还是人——结果总是很糟。这是史上评价最差的主意之一，和给机器人和计算机赋予人类感情，或者回到过去只改变一件小事一样糟糕。

00:46:39.400 --> 00:46:43.350
<v Ira Glass>The next day, we were finally allowed to see Ralph in his hospital room.
<v Ira Glass>第二天，我们终于被允许在病房里见到拉尔夫。

00:46:43.350 --> 00:46:45.090
<v Ira Glass>So Ralph, how are you feeling?
<v Ira Glass>拉尔夫，你感觉怎么样？

00:46:45.090 --> 00:46:56.830
<v Ralph Fisher>Oh, I feel good. They had me up walking this morning, first thing. I've walked the block in the hospital about four times now, and the last two without any help.
<v Ralph Fisher>哦，我感觉很好。今天一早他们就让我下床走路了。我已经围着医院走了大约四圈，最后两圈还没人帮忙。

00:46:56.830 --> 00:47:02.460
<v Ira Glass>There was a scar on his nose. His lip and chin were busted. His spine, we found out later, had a hairline fracture.
<v Ira Glass>他的鼻梁上有疤。他的嘴唇和下巴被打肿了。我们后来发现他的脊椎有一处细微骨折。

00:47:02.460 --> 00:47:14.280
<v Ira Glass>So you were saying when we talked before that you felt like, with Second Chance, what you've got is 95% of what Chance was. And I wonder if, after this incident, you might downgrade him to just, like, 80%, or 70%?
<v Ira Glass>所以你之前谈到，你觉得有了“第二次机会”，你找回了“机会”95%的特质。我想知道，发生这件事后，你是否会把它降级为80%或70%？

00:47:14.280 --> 00:47:24.820
<v Ralph Fisher>[LAUGHING] No, because this is exactly the same type incident as when he butted me 18 months ago, a year and a half ago, when he--
<v Ralph Fisher>[大笑] 不会，因为这和我18个月前被它顶伤的那次完全是一样的事件。

00:47:24.820 --> 00:47:31.490
<v Ira Glass>Come on, you think, really, 95% after that?
<v Ira Glass>算了吧，真的，经过那次之后你还觉得是95%？

00:47:31.490 --> 00:47:48.300
<v Ralph Fisher>I'm just thinking that, you know, we just have to have a lot of faith in things to work out. So I forgive him, you know? I just shouldn't have been that close.
<v Ralph Fisher>我只是在想，你知道，我们必须对事情的发展充满信心。所以我原谅它，你知道吗？我只是不该离得那么近。

00:47:48.300 --> 00:47:57.560
<v Ira Glass>Yeah. What would Second Chance have to do to convince you he's never going to be like Chance? Like, how many times would he have to attack you?
<v Ira Glass>是的。“第二次机会”必须做些什么才能让你确信它永远不会像“机会”？比如说，它必须攻击你多少次？

00:47:57.560 --> 00:48:07.030
<v Ralph Fisher>After he's seven, then he has to attack me. Then the lines will be drawn in the sand, like we were discussing.
<v Ralph Fisher>等它七岁之后，它要是再攻击我，那底线就画好了，就像我们讨论的那样。

00:48:07.030 --> 00:48:09.620
<v Ira Glass>Yeah. You still have hope.
<v Ira Glass>是的。你仍然抱有希望。

00:48:09.620 --> 00:48:16.700
<v Ralph Fisher>Oh, yeah. Surely, yeah. I've been having fun here, and-- Sandra, hand me that horse, would you?
<v Ralph Fisher>哦，当然，是的。我在这里玩得很开心，而且……桑德拉，把那匹马递给我，好吗？

00:48:16.700 --> 00:48:20.700
<v Sandra Fisher>Sandra hands him this little toy horse that one of their friends brought Ralph.
<v Sandra Fisher>桑德拉递给他那个朋友带给拉尔夫的小玩具马。

00:48:20.700 --> 00:48:21.340
<v Ralph Fisher>How does it work?
<v Ralph Fisher>它是怎么用的？

00:48:21.340 --> 00:48:24.300
<v Ralph Fisher>[GALLOPING SOUND]
<v Ralph Fisher>[奔跑声]

00:48:24.300 --> 00:48:25.780
<v Ralph Fisher>[NEIGHING]
<v Ralph Fisher>[嘶鸣声]

00:48:25.780 --> 00:48:28.740
<v Ralph Fisher>[RALPH LAUGHS]
<v Ralph Fisher>[拉尔夫大笑]

00:48:28.740 --> 00:48:31.200
<v Ralph Fisher>[GALLOPING SOUND]
<v Ralph Fisher>[奔跑声]

00:48:31.200 --> 00:48:35.760
<v Ralph Fisher>[NEIGHING]
<v Ralph Fisher>[嘶鸣声]

00:48:35.760 --> 00:48:51.560
<v Ralph Fisher>That's how I feel right now, you know? He-he-he-he-he-he-he. I'm going to walk out of here tomorrow or the next day, and I'll go right back out there and give him another bucket of feed. I mean, that's what you have to do.
<v Ralph Fisher>我现在感觉就是这样，你知道吗？哈哈哈哈。我明天或后天就会走出这里，我会直接回到那里给它再喂一桶饲料。我是说，你必须这么做。

00:48:51.560 --> 00:48:52.550
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>是的。

00:48:52.550 --> 00:49:08.390
<v Ralph Fisher>If it was easy, there'd be a bunch of kids out there taking care of Brahman bulls.
<v Ralph Fisher>如果这很容易，就会有很多孩子在照顾婆罗门公牛了。

00:49:08.390 --> 00:49:27.830
<v Ira Glass>Well, that happened back in 2005, which was when this episode was first broadcast. Ralph turned out to be right. As Second Chance got older, he did become tamer and more trustworthy, but never as docile as Chance was. Sandra and Ralph always had to be careful with him. And in March of 2008, eight years old, Second Chance died.
<v Ira Glass>嗯，那是2005年发生的，那是这集节目首次播出的时候。拉尔夫确实是对的。随着“第二次机会”长大，它确实变得更温顺、更可靠了，但从来没有像“机会”那么驯服。桑德拉和拉尔夫一直不得不对它保持小心。2008年3月，“第二次机会”去世了，享年八岁。

00:49:27.830 --> 00:49:46.730
<v Ira Glass>In an email to friends, Sandra wrote, "To think that this huge animal who's put Ralph in the hospital twice will be so missed-- it never occurred to us that by having a clone we would lose him twice." Sandra said that before his death, Texas A&M collected some tissue, which opened the door for a possibility of a Third Chance.
<v Ira Glass>在一封给朋友的邮件中，桑德拉写道：“想到这头巨大的动物曾两次把拉尔夫送进医院，却还是这么让人怀念——我们从未想到，通过克隆，我们竟然会失去它两次。”桑德拉说，在它去世前，德州农工大学采集了一些组织，这打开了第三次机会的可能性。

00:49:46.730 --> 00:50:06.720
<v Ira Glass>This story, by the way, is one that we did for our TV show back when we had a series on Showtime. If you're curious to see Chance, and Second Chance, and Ralph, and Sandra, you can find the series at Showtime, or Amazon Prime, or iTunes.
<v Ira Glass>顺便说一句，这个故事是我们以前在Showtime系列节目时制作的。如果你好奇想看看“机会”、“第二次机会”、拉尔夫和桑德拉，你可以在Showtime、亚马逊Prime或iTunes上找到这个系列。

00:50:06.720 --> 00:50:21.330
<v Ira Glass>Act Three, "French Kiss." We end our program today with this story from Sara Vowell of a man reunited with a country. People lined the roads to see him wherever he traveled. Newspaper accounts called it "a delirium of feeling." Here's Sarah.
<v Ira Glass>第三幕，“法式热吻”。今天我们以莎拉·沃威尔讲述的一个男人与国家重聚的故事结束我们的节目。人们排在道路两旁看他旅行经过。报纸报道称这是一种“情感的狂热”。这是莎拉的报道。

00:50:21.330 --> 00:51:06.400
<v Sarah Vowell>On August 16, 1824, an elderly French gentleman sailed into New York Harbor, and giddy Americans were there to welcome him-- or rather, to welcome him back. It had been 30 years since the Revolutionary War hero, the Marquis de Lafayette, had last set foot in the United States. And he was so beloved, so universally revered, that the frenzy, the ardor of his reception has yet to be matched. 80,000 people showed up to cheer for Lafayette-- 80,000. The entire population of New York at the time was 120,000.
<v Sarah Vowell>1824年8月16日，一位年长的法国绅士航行进入纽约港，兴奋的美国人在那里欢迎他——或者说，欢迎他归来。距离独立战争英雄、拉法耶特侯爵上次踏上美国土地已经30年了。他是如此受爱戴，如此普遍受尊敬，以至于他接待时的那种狂热和激情至今无人能比。8万民众现身为拉法耶特欢呼——8万。纽约当时的总人口也才12万。

00:51:06.400 --> 00:51:28.860
<v Sarah Vowell>When Lafayette first got here in 1777, he was only 19 years old. A wealthy, orphaned aristocrat, he charmed his way into a commission in George Washington's Continental Army as an unpaid volunteer. He was a relentless soldier, almost freakishly brave, disciplined and oozing panache all at once.
<v Sarah Vowell>拉法耶特1777年第一次来到这里时，年仅19岁。作为一位富有的孤儿贵族，他通过魅力获得乔治·华盛顿大陆军的军衔，担任无薪志愿者。他是一位无情的士兵，几乎勇敢得不可思议，纪律严明，同时又风度翩翩。

00:51:28.860 --> 00:52:04.030
<v Sarah Vowell>He rode a white horse, was wounded in the Battle of Brandywine, led the liberation of Virginia, and suffered through a winter at Valley Forge, living among his fellow soldiers in a crummy hut, though he could have easily afforded to wait for spring in a mansion the size of Newark. His troops adored him. George Washington considered him his adopted son. Alexander Hamilton wrote him letters that read like mash notes.
<v Sarah Vowell>他骑着白马，在布兰迪万战役中受伤，领导了解放弗吉尼亚的行动，并在福吉谷的冬天忍受了磨难，和战友们住在一起，尽管他完全负担得起在像纽瓦克那么大的豪宅里等待春天。他的军队崇拜他。乔治·华盛顿视他为养子。亚历山大·汉密尔顿写给他的信读起来就像情书。

00:52:04.030 --> 00:52:49.520
<v Sarah Vowell>Invited by his old friend, President Monroe, to be the nation's guest on the eve of the 50th anniversary of American independence, Lafayette's trip here amounted to a euphoric 13-month victory lap around the country in which he visited all of the then-24 states. Everywhere Lafayette went, he was serenaded by music composed in his honor-- "Hail Lafayette," "Lafayette's March," "The Lafayette Waltz," "The Lafayette Rondo," and in decreasing geographical order, the songs, "Lafayette's Welcome to North America," "Lafayette's Welcome to the United States," "Lafayette's Welcome to New York," and "Lafayette's Welcome to Philadelphia."
<v Sarah Vowell>受老友门罗总统的邀请，在美国独立50周年前夕作为国家的客人，拉法耶特的此次行程相当于一场长达13个月、令人陶醉的环国胜利巡回赛，他访问了当时所有的24个州。无论拉法耶特走到哪里，他都会听到为纪念他而创作的音乐——《拉法耶特万岁》、《拉法耶特进行曲》、《拉法耶特圆舞曲》、《拉法耶特回旋曲》，按地理顺序依次有歌曲《拉法耶特欢迎来到北美》、《拉法耶特欢迎来到美国》、《拉法耶特欢迎来到纽约》和《拉法耶特欢迎来到费城》。

00:52:49.520 --> 00:53:09.840
<v Sarah Vowell>Meanwhile, the souvenir trade cranked out an unprecedented pile of Lafayette-themed knickknacks from ladies' gloves to dishes. How many times must he have reached for a cookie and seen his own eyes staring back at him from a commemorative plate?
<v Sarah Vowell>与此同时，纪念品贸易推出了前所未有的以拉法耶特为主题的小饰品，从女士手套到餐具。他到底有多少次伸手去拿饼干，看到自己的眼睛在纪念盘上盯着自己？

00:53:09.840 --> 00:53:44.340
<v Sarah Vowell>In Brooklyn, Lafayette was invited to lay the cornerstone of a new Children's Library. At the ceremony, he picked up and kissed a neighborhood kid, the five-year-old Walt Whitman. In Philadelphia, the French chef in charge of preparing the-- count 'em-- 60 courses for Lafayette's banquet dropped dead in his kitchen that very morning, felled by overwork and the steam heat of simmering sauces.
<v Sarah Vowell>在布鲁克林，拉法耶特被邀请为一座新的儿童图书馆奠基。在仪式上，他抱起并亲吻了一名邻家小孩，也就是五岁的沃尔特·惠特曼。在费城，负责准备——数数吧——60道菜的宴会的法国厨师，就在当天早上在厨房里猝死了，倒在过劳和酱汁炖煮的热气下。

00:53:44.340 --> 00:54:07.740
<v Sarah Vowell>And speaking of Philadelphia, if you have visited what came to be called Independence Hall, you have Lafayette to thank. The building where the Declaration of Independence and the Constitution were signed was decrepit and abandoned. It was only when Philadelphians needed a symbolically appropriate place for Lafayette's reception did they remember the old hall and sweep up.
<v Sarah Vowell>说到费城，如果你访问过所谓的“独立厅”，你得感谢拉法耶特。那个签署《独立宣言》和《宪法》的建筑曾破败不堪、无人问津。只有当费城人需要一个象征性合适的地方来接待拉法耶特时，他们才想起旧厅并打扫干净。

00:54:07.740 --> 00:54:51.990
<v Sarah Vowell>When Lafayette waded through the crowd of 20,000 locals there to greet him and crossed the-- to him-- hallowed threshold, he preached, "Within these sacred walls, by a council of wise and devoted patriots, was boldly declared the independence of these vast United States, which has begun for the civilized world the era of a new and of the only true social order founded on the inalienable rights of man." Notice the melancholy in that word, "only", when he hails American government as the "only" true social order founded on the inalienable rights of man.
<v Sarah Vowell>当拉法耶特穿过2万当地人迎接他的队伍，跨过对他来说神圣的门槛时，他感叹道：“在这神圣的墙内，由一群英明、忠诚的爱国者组成的议会，大胆宣布了这些庞大的合众国的独立，这为文明世界开启了一个新的，也是唯一真正的社会秩序时代，建立在人不可剥夺的权利之上。”注意到他在称赞美国政府是建立在人不可剥夺权利之上的“唯一”真正社会秩序时，话语中流露出的忧郁了吗？

00:54:51.990 --> 00:55:31.040
<v Sarah Vowell>After the French Revolution of 1789, Lafayette not only watched his hopes for American-style civil liberties in his homeland get chopped into thousands of guillotined pieces during the Reign of Terror, he subsequently spent five years in a royal Austrian prison and lived through the restoration of the French king, the dictatorship of Napoleon, and the restoration of yet another French king. So Lafayette, more than anyone, was aware of the uniqueness and the importance of the American republic.
<v Sarah Vowell>1789年法国大革命后，拉法耶特不仅目睹了他在祖国实现美国式公民自由的希望在恐怖统治期间被送上断头台，切成碎片，他还先后在奥地利皇家监狱度过了五年，历经了法国国王复辟、拿破仑独裁，以及另一个法国国王的复辟。所以，拉法耶特比任何人都了解美国共和国的独特性和重要性。

00:55:31.040 --> 00:56:00.710
<v Sarah Vowell>At that point, American democracy had descended from its early idealism into the kind of messy partisan backbiting we're familiar with today. Lafayette's arrival in 1824 coincides with one of the most contentious presidential elections in American history, with no clear electoral college winner between John Quincy Adams and Andrew Jackson, with Adams assuming the presidency under suspicions some labeled "the corrupt bargain."
<v Sarah Vowell>在那时，美国民主已经从早期的理想主义跌落到我们今天熟悉的混乱的党派抨击中。拉法耶特1824年的到来恰逢美国历史上最激烈的总统选举之一，约翰·昆西·亚当斯和安德鲁·杰克逊之间没有明确的选举团获胜者，亚当斯在“腐败交易”的怀疑中出任总统。

00:56:00.710 --> 00:56:48.980
<v Sarah Vowell>Meanwhile, Congress had just fought its first epic battle over slavery, the fight that led to the Missouri Compromise, the fight that would soon enough lead to the Civil War. But Lafayette, belonging to neither North nor South, to no political party or faction, was a walking, talking reminder of the sacrifices and bravery of the revolutionary generation and what they wanted this country to be. His return was not just a reunion with his beloved Americans, it was a reunion for Americans with their own astonishing, singular past.
<v Sarah Vowell>与此同时，国会刚刚进行了第一次关于奴隶制的史诗级斗争，这场斗争导致了《密苏里妥协》，这场斗争很快就会导致内战。但拉法耶特不属于北方也不属于南方，不属于任何政党或派系，他是一个行走的、谈话的提醒者，提醒人们革命一代的牺牲和勇敢，以及他们想要这个国家变成什么样。他的回归不仅仅是与他所爱的美国人重聚，更是美国人与他们自己令人惊叹、独特的过去的一次重聚。

00:56:48.980 --> 00:57:22.330
<v Sarah Vowell>On New Year's Day, 1825, Congress threw him yet another of his nightly banquets. And Speaker of the House, Henry Clay, toasted Lafayette as a great apostle of Liberty. In reply, Lafayette toasted back with a prophecy-- "To the perpetual union of the United States. It has always served us in times of storm. One day, it will save the world."
<v Sarah Vowell>1825年元旦，国会再次为他举办了晚宴。众议院议长亨利·克莱将拉法耶特敬酒为伟大的自由使徒。拉法耶特以预言回敬——“为了合众国的永久统一。它总是在暴风雨时刻服务于我们。有一天，它将拯救世界。”

00:57:22.330 --> 00:58:05.270
<v Sarah Vowell>And he was right. Every so often, America has saved the world. Of course, we muck it up every now and then, as well. But in 1917, after American Expeditionary Forces crossed the Atlantic to aid France in World War I, General Pershing marched his troops to Lafayette's Paris grave where the old soldier had been buried under dirt from Bunker Hill. They placed an American flag into that American dirt. Lafayette, one of them said, we are here.
<v Sarah Vowell>他是对的。每隔一段时间，美国确实拯救了世界。当然，我们也时不时搞砸，正如偶尔所见。但在1917年，美国远征军横跨大西洋支援法国一战后，潘兴将军带领军队行进到拉法耶特位于巴黎的坟墓，这位老兵被埋葬在来自邦克山土壤之下。他们将一面美国国旗插入那片美国土壤中。其中一人说，拉法耶特，我们到了。

00:58:05.270 --> 00:58:11.910
<v Ira Glass>Sarah Vowell. Her book about Lafayette's experiences in America is called, Lafayette in the Somewhat United States.
<v Ira Glass>莎拉·沃威尔。她关于拉法耶特在美国经历的书名为《有点联合的美国里的拉法耶特》。

00:58:11.910 --> 00:58:12.390
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC PLAYING - MASE, "WELCOME BACK"]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐播放 - Mase，《欢迎回来》]

00:58:12.390 --> 00:58:13.360
<v Mase>(SINGING) Welcome back.
<v Mase>(演唱) 欢迎回来。

00:58:13.360 --> 00:58:18.670
<v Man 2>Oh, hey, man, how you doin'? You good? I know you like that.
<v Man 2>哦，嘿，伙计，你怎么样？还好吗？我知道你喜欢那个。

00:58:18.670 --> 00:58:19.160
<v Mase>(SINGING) Welcome back.
<v Mase>(演唱) 欢迎回来。

00:58:19.160 --> 00:58:38.590
<v Ira Glass>Well, our program was produced today by Alex Blumberg and myself, with Diane Cook, Jane Marie, Sarah Koenig, and Lisa Pollock Our senior producer for today's show was Julie Snyder. Additional production on today's rerun from Jessica Lussenhop, Katherine Rae Mondo, Stowe Nelson, and Matt Tierney. Special thanks today to Chandra Conway for bringing us this story of Second Chance, also to Chris Wilcha and Jenny Golden.
<v Ira Glass>今天我们的节目由亚历克斯·布伦伯格和我本人制作，黛安·库克、简·玛丽、莎拉·科尼格和丽莎·波洛克参与。今天节目的资深制作人是朱莉·斯奈德。今天重播的额外制作由杰西卡·卢森霍普、凯瑟琳·雷·蒙多、斯托·尼尔森和马特·蒂尔尼完成。今天特别感谢钱德拉·康威为我们带来这个“第二次机会”的故事，也感谢克里斯·威尔查和珍妮·戈尔登。

